Tests to perform before every commit:-
python -m compileall app
ruff check .
ruff check --fix
ruff format --check .
ruff format .
python -m pytest
python -m pytest tests\unit -v
npm run lint
npm run build





3. ACTION_SEND_EMAIL
ACTION_SEND_EMAIL = "SEND_EMAIL"

Meaning:

Send the customer an email related to the recovery.

For example:

Payment failed
      ↓
AI decides:
SEND_EMAIL
      ↓
Send email:
"Your payment could not be completed.
Please update your payment method."

This could eventually integrate with:

Resend
SendGrid
AWS SES
another email provider

But you don't necessarily need to fully implement this for your MVP.

The important thing is that your architecture has a place for this action.






Part 12 — Frontend polish + shared UX

Before touching FastAPI, we'll make the current frontend more production-ready:

1. Global loading behavior
2. Empty states
3. Error states
4. Skeleton loaders
5. Button states
6. Consistent badges
7. Consistent page headers
8. Mobile navigation verification
9. Accessibility pass
10. Final horizontal-overflow audit
11. 404 page
12. Global error boundary
13. Final component cleanup

Then your frontend will be in a very good state:

                    REVRECOVER FRONTEND
                           │
              ┌────────────┴────────────┐
              │                         │
        PRODUCT UI                INTEGRATION
              │                         │
              ▼                         ▼
       All pages complete        API architecture ready
              │                         │
              └────────────┬────────────┘
                           │
                           ▼
                     BACKEND PHASE
                           │
                           ▼
                        FastAPI
                           │
                           ▼
                      PostgreSQL

And only when your FastAPI backend is actually implemented will we start replacing the mock services with real API calls, endpoint by endpoint.





